course: language
lesson: 21
title: British and American words
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Britain and America speak the same language, English, but over about 400 years each side picked some different words for the same everyday things.
explain: Picture Emma from London in a New York café asking for "chips": she gets a bag of crispy slices, because in America those are "chips" and her hot potato chips are "fries".
explain: Neither side is wrong: "colour" is right in Britain and "color" is right in America, just as "lift" and "elevator" are both proper words for the same machine.
explain: Some "American" words are really old British ones: "fall" for autumn and "soccer" for football both started in England and simply stayed alive across the ocean.
explain: The trick is to know both words and use the one your listener knows, like a kid who speaks two kinds of English.
fact: British "chips" are American "fries"; British "crisps" are American "chips".
fact: In America "pants" means trousers; in Britain "pants" means underwear, so an American praising your "pants" means your trousers.
fact: The path beside a road is a "pavement" in Britain and a "sidewalk" in America.
fact: American spelling dropped the u from words like "color" and "favorite", helped by Noah Webster's dictionary of 1828; Britain kept "colour" and "favourite"; both spellings are correct in their own country.
fact: A British "biscuit" is an American "cookie"; an American "biscuit" is a soft bread roll a bit like a scone.
fact: Americans say "Mom", most of Britain says "Mum", and parts of Northern England, Wales and Ireland say "Mam".
fact: "Soccer" was made up by students in England in the 1800s, from "association football"; Americans kept it to tell it apart from American football.
fact: "Fall" for autumn was used in England in the 1500s and 1600s, from "the fall of the leaf"; settlers took it to America, and Britain mostly switched to "autumn".
fact: The letter Z is "zed" in Britain, Ireland, Canada, Australia and most English-speaking places, and "zee" in the USA.
fact: More pairs, British first: lift and elevator, torch and flashlight, queue and line, petrol and gas, holiday and vacation, flat and apartment.
fact: Britain, Ireland and Australia say "maths"; the USA and Canada say "math"; both are short for "mathematics".
fact: In America the "first floor" is the floor at street level; in Britain that is the "ground floor", and the British "first floor" is one floor up.
exercise: Emma from London asks for "chips" in New York and gets a bag of crisps. Why? | In America "chips" means crisps | The café made a mistake with her order | New York has no hot potato chips | Americans call every snack "chips"
exercise: What do Americans call British hot chips? | Fries | Crisps | Wedges | Chips
exercise: Jake from Texas says "I love your pants!" to his London cousin. What did he mean? | He liked her trousers | He liked her underwear | He liked her pancakes | He liked her pens
exercise: What is the British word for an American "sidewalk"? | Pavement | Roadway | Footbridge | Kerbside
exercise: Isla writes "colour" and her pen pal in America writes "color". Who is right? | Both, each in their own country | Only Isla, because British came first | Only the pen pal, because it is shorter | Neither, it should be "culler"
exercise: Whose dictionary of 1828 helped spread American spellings like "color"? | Noah Webster's | William Shakespeare's | Charles Dickens's | Queen Victoria's
exercise: Charlie from Ohio wants a "cookie". What would Charlie from Manchester call it? | A biscuit | A scone | A muffin | A cracker
exercise: Where did the word "soccer" start? | In England, from "association football" | In America, as a brand-new word for the game | In Spain, from an old word meaning a kicked ball | In Australia, as surfer slang
exercise: Why does American English say "fall" for autumn? | Settlers took the old English word and kept it | Americans invented it in the 1900s for falling leaves | It comes from a Native American word for the season | It was a spelling mistake in a newspaper
exercise: Leo from Texas says "zee". What does his cousin in Leeds call the letter Z? | Zed | Zee | Zet | Izzard
exercise: Tom asks for the "torch". What does his American cousin call it? | A flashlight | A lantern | A spotlight | A candle
exercise: Which pair has the British word first and the American word second? | Queue and line | Elevator and lift | Gas and petrol | Vacation and holiday
exercise: Sam says "maths" and his American friend says "math". What are both short for? | Mathematics | Mathematical tests | Matching | Maths-and-sums
exercise: Chloe's American aunt says "meet on the first floor". Where will the aunt wait? | At street level | One floor up | In the basement | On the roof
exercise: Freya's mum asks "Shall we take the lift?" in a London hotel. What would an American say instead? | The elevator | The escalator | The stairs | The lift-up
exercise: Which group of countries says "maths"? | Britain, Ireland and Australia | The USA and Canada | The USA and Britain | Only Scotland
test: Your mate goes "soccer is a dumb American word, innit, it's FOOTBALL bruv." What's true? | "Soccer" was made up in England in the 1800s | He's right, Americans invented it last year | "Soccer" comes from the French word for sock | It was invented by a TV advert
test: Gran reckons "fall" is "lazy new American talk". What's the wise reply? | "Fall" is an old English word that America kept | She's right, it began in the 1990s on American telly | It's slang that American films spread around the world | It's a spelling mistake for "fell"
test: In a cartoon, an American kid says "pavement" and points at the road itself. Could that be right? | Yes, in America "pavement" can mean the road surface | No, Americans never use the word "pavement" at all, ever | No, in America the pavement means only the grass verge | Yes, but only in Canada
test: Your Yank cousin ("Yank" is British slang for an American) says "zed is a made-up British joke". What do you tell him? | Most English-speaking countries say "zed" | He's right, only the Queen said "zed" | "Zed" is only used in maths | Both names are wrong, it's "zet"
test: You're in Boston and your mate texts "meet u ground floor lol". What should you say to an American to find it? | The first floor | The second floor | The basement | The top floor
test: Your teacher in Leeds marks "color" wrong in your British essay. Is the teacher being unfair? | No, in a British essay use British spelling | Yes, American spelling is newer and so always better | Yes, spelling never matters as long as people understand | No, because "color" is wrong everywhere
test: Kai's mate from Alabama says "y'all want biscuits with gravy?" ("Y'all" means you all.) What will arrive? | Soft bread rolls with gravy | Chocolate cookies with gravy | Crisps with gravy | Crackers with jam
test: What would happen if a British boy in New York asked a shop for "a rubber"? (In Britain a rubber is for rubbing out pencil.) | He might be misunderstood, because Americans say "eraser" | Nothing, because every American also says "rubber" for a pencil eraser | He would be handed a car tyre, because that's what rubber means there | The shop would give him a ball
test: Hollie reckons Brits and Americans "basically can't understand each other, it's two languages". Is she right? | No, most differences are small word pairs that are easy to learn | Yes, they need interpreters to talk, just like French and English speakers do | Yes, but only on the phone | No, because there are no differences at all between them
test: Ella from Leeds says "Mam, can I have a biscuit?" Her friend says "Mam" is wrong. What's true? | "Mam" is normal in parts of Northern England, Wales and Ireland | "Mam" is a typo for "Mum" that spread because of phones autocorrecting | "Mam" is only used for grandmas, never for mothers, in any part of Britain | "Mam" is American slang
test: Your American cousin says "I'm out of gas". You are in the car. What has run out? | Petrol, the fuel for the car | Air in the tyres | Cooking gas at home | Fizzy drink
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the divergence between British and American English vocabulary? | Each variety employs certain distinct words for identical objects, and both are correct | American usage constitutes an erroneous corruption of British usage, to be corrected | British usage is regarded as obsolete in all respects by the authorities of both nations | The two varieties are mutually unintelligible
exam: Identify the origin of the term "soccer", given that it is now chiefly employed in the United States. | It was coined in England as an abbreviation of "association football" | It was devised in the United States in the twentieth century by sports journalists | It derives from a Spanish term for a ball | It originated as a commercial trade name for a brand of leather football
exam: A person residing in the United States proposes a meeting "on the first floor". Ascertain the location so designated in British terms. | The ground floor | The first floor | The second floor | The basement
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the spellings "colour" and "color". | Each is the accepted form within its respective country | "Color" is the sole correct form worldwide since 1828 | "Colour" was abolished by Noah Webster's dictionary of 1828 | Neither appears in any dictionary
